<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>A box holds <m>{8}</m> blue pens and <m>{3}</m> red pens. Two pens are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement.</p><p>Give each probability exactly, as a fraction or an exact decimal.</p><p>(a) The first pen is blue. What is the probability that the second is blue?</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/>  </div><p>(b) Find the probability that both are blue.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/>  </div><p>(c) Find the probability that one is blue and one is red.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans3"/>  </div><p>(d) Find the probability that at least one is red.</p><div><fillin characters="8" name="ans4"/>  </div></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>Without replacement, count what is left at the second stage: one pen has gone, so there are <m>{10}</m> left. The four paths of the tree are</p><tabular border="1" style="border-collapse:collapse;text-align:center;"><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">First</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Second</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">Probability of the path</cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">blue</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">blue</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>\dfrac{{8}}{{11}} \times \dfrac{{7}}{{10}} = {\frac{28}{55}}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">blue</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">red</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>\dfrac{{8}}{{11}} \times \dfrac{{3}}{{10}} = {\frac{12}{55}}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">red</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">blue</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>\dfrac{{3}}{{11}} \times \dfrac{{8}}{{10}} = {\frac{12}{55}}</m></cell></row><row><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">red</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;">red</cell><cell style="padding:4px 10px;"><m>\dfrac{{3}}{{11}} \times \dfrac{{2}}{{10}} = {\frac{3}{55}}</m></cell></row></tabular><p>(a) After a blue pen is taken, <m>{7}</m> of the <m>{10}</m> left are blue: <m>{\frac{7}{10}}</m>.</p><p>(b) <m>\dfrac{{8}}{{11}} \times \dfrac{{7}}{{10}} = {\frac{28}{55}}</m>.</p><p>(c) Two paths: blue then red, or red then blue. <m>{\frac{12}{55}} + {\frac{12}{55}} = {\frac{24}{55}}</m>.</p><p>(d) <q>At least one red</q> is everything except both blue: <m>1 - {\frac{28}{55}} = {\frac{27}{55}}</m>. The four paths add to 1.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{7}{10}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{28}{55}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{24}{55}</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>\frac{27}{55}</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>